#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065362 :tutorial/content "Consider a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points $x=a$ and $x=b$. The **average rate of change** of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in[a,b]$ is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{\\text{change in}y}{\\text{change in}x} = \\dfrac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the average rate of change represents the slope of the **secant line** which passes through $(a,f(a))$ and $(b,f(b))$.\n\n![](images/2d/2d27bcc73d1bcb8545c058c06553e3f4bc80d5426a98f5bb90e4d4c0aef71108.png)\n\n**Note:** In general, a secant is a line that intersects a curve at two or more distinct points." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e32384d-35c4-578e-b254-9cf90914529e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 312 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
